WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.67
This is journey sixteen of Hewn, the software factory: inspect versioned artifacts.

00:00:06.67 --> 00:00:11.65
Every stage writes its output to the product's repository: the brief, the mocks,

00:00:11.65 --> 00:00:14.70
the design system, the requirements, the designs.

00:00:14.70 --> 00:00:19.87
Owners and Viewers want to open each of those at the exact commit a stage produced.

00:00:19.87 --> 00:00:23.98
This video walks through how it was built: the artifacts endpoint,

00:00:23.98 --> 00:00:28.47
the rules that decide what counts as an artifact, the new Artifacts tab,

00:00:28.47 --> 00:00:29.34
and the tests.

00:00:30.49 --> 00:00:35.52
A user opens a project's Artifacts and sees each stage's artifacts,

00:00:35.52 --> 00:00:36.95
newest stage first.

00:00:36.95 --> 00:00:42.06
Every row has the path, the version as a short commit SHA, the time,

00:00:42.06 --> 00:00:46.64
and a link that opens the file on GitHub at that very commit.

00:00:47.79 --> 00:00:48.97
Here is the flow.

00:00:48.97 --> 00:00:52.58
Workers already commit through Hewn's GitHub broker,

00:00:52.58 --> 00:00:56.61
which records each commit's SHA and paths against the run.

00:00:56.61 --> 00:00:58.90
So there is no separate recorder.

00:00:58.90 --> 00:01:03.69
Once a run succeeds, its commit is the version of the files it holds.

00:01:03.69 --> 00:01:09.45
When the tab asks, the API reads the commits of succeeded runs and shapes them into

00:01:09.45 --> 00:01:09.93
stages.

00:01:11.08 --> 00:01:14.63
The handler keeps only commits from runs that succeeded,

00:01:14.63 --> 00:01:19.12
so a failed attempt never becomes a version, and a retry's commit does.

00:01:19.12 --> 00:01:22.72
Commits to the scaffold or feature branches are left out,

00:01:22.72 --> 00:01:26.39
because the default branch is what the artifacts describe.

00:01:26.39 --> 00:01:31.39
The decision log every stage appends to has its own tab, so it is left out too.

00:01:31.39 --> 00:01:34.87
Each path's version is the latest commit that wrote it,

00:01:34.87 --> 00:01:36.70
and stages come newest first.

00:01:37.05 --> 00:01:40.65
Stages write whole folders, such as a page per mock.

00:01:40.65 --> 00:01:46.12
Files inside a subfolder of the stage's output folder fold into that subfolder,

00:01:46.12 --> 00:01:49.17
which links to GitHub as a tree, not a blob.

00:01:49.17 --> 00:01:53.74
So stage two lists one row per page folder, not hundreds of files.

00:01:53.74 --> 00:01:59.35
Commit records now also keep their branch, so the read model can tell them apart.

00:02:00.50 --> 00:02:05.45
In Mission Control, each stage is a section headed by its number and name.

00:02:05.45 --> 00:02:11.13
Each row shows the path, the short SHA, and the time, as minutes ago within the hour,

00:02:11.13 --> 00:02:12.67
then as a day and time.

00:02:12.67 --> 00:02:17.88
The link opens in a new tab, and its accessible name says which file and which

00:02:17.88 --> 00:02:18.95
commit it opens.

00:02:18.95 --> 00:02:22.90
The tab reads the artifacts again whenever a stage changes,

00:02:22.90 --> 00:02:25.37
so new files appear without a reload.

00:02:26.52 --> 00:02:31.62
The backend tests came first, and failed with not found: there was no route.

00:02:31.62 --> 00:02:35.78
Four tests now cover the stages newest first with their links,

00:02:35.78 --> 00:02:39.41
folders folding into trees, the decision log left out,

00:02:39.41 --> 00:02:44.78
a failed run's commit replaced by the retry's, branch commits left out, Viewers,

00:02:44.78 --> 00:02:46.32
and an unknown project.

00:02:46.67 --> 00:02:51.05
End to end, a live test opens the Artifacts tab on a new project,

00:02:51.05 --> 00:02:56.71
starts a real worker, and waits for the brief to appear with its seven character SHA

00:02:56.71 --> 00:03:00.69
and a link to GitHub at that full commit, without a reload.

00:03:01.04 --> 00:03:04.99
All green, the backend has two hundred and thirty two tests,

00:03:04.99 --> 00:03:07.49
and the frontend three new view tests.

00:03:07.49 --> 00:03:12.96
End to end, there are eleven new tests: three for behaviour, one live pipeline run,

00:03:12.96 --> 00:03:17.63
three visual comparisons with the mocks, and four accessibility checks.

00:03:18.78 --> 00:03:20.20
Here is Kiln Booking.

00:03:20.20 --> 00:03:24.25
The Artifacts tab is open and empty while the worker starts.

00:03:24.25 --> 00:03:29.93
Then the stages succeed, and their files arrive, each with the commit that holds it.

00:03:29.93 --> 00:03:35.53
The brief links to GitHub at its commit, and the skills folders from bootstrap fold

00:03:35.53 --> 00:03:36.75
into one row each.

00:03:37.10 --> 00:03:42.22
As a Viewer the tab reads the same, in light and in Mission Control's dark mode,

00:03:42.22 --> 00:03:43.18
and on a phone.

00:03:43.53 --> 00:03:47.57
A project no stage has written to yet says there are no artifacts,

00:03:47.57 --> 00:03:50.08
while a failure says why, with try again.

00:03:51.23 --> 00:03:53.17
Journey sixteen is complete.

00:03:53.17 --> 00:03:57.47
Every stage's output is versioned by the commit that holds it,

00:03:57.47 --> 00:04:01.56
taken from the broker's own records of runs that succeeded.

00:04:01.56 --> 00:04:06.41
The Artifacts tab lists them newest stage first, with path, short SHA,

00:04:06.41 --> 00:04:09.26
time and a link to GitHub at that commit.

00:04:09.26 --> 00:04:11.89
And it stays current as stages finish.

00:04:11.89 --> 00:04:13.90
Next comes journey seventeen.
